  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Explained\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding an item in a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which would take O(n) time), binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half:\n\n1. Look at the middle element.\n2. If the middle element is the target, you're done.\n3. If the target is smaller, search the left half.\n4. If the target is larger, search the right half.\n5. Repeat until the target is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThis gives a **time complexity of O(log n)**, which is dramatically faster than linear search for large lists.\n\n## What is `lower_bound`?\n\n`lower_bound` finds the **first index** where the target could be inserted while keeping the list sorted. In other words, it returns the index of the **first element that is greater than or equal to** the target. If all elements are smaller than the target, it returns the length of the list.\n\nThis is useful for:\n- Inserting elements into a sorted list while maintaining order.\n- Finding the first occurrence of a value in a list",
